::CW_U06_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Story problem: Readers do not understand why Ellis leaves at the end. Which revision best fits?{
~A. Change Ellis's shirt color.#Incorrect. This does not clarify motivation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Add random weather description.#Incorrect. Weather does not automatically solve character motivation.
~C. Delete every character.#Incorrect. This removes the story instead of revising it.
=D. Add one action showing Ellis choosing to leave before the final line.#Correct. This directly clarifies motivation and sequence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Story problem: A suspense scene moves too quickly. Which revision best improves pacing?{
~A. Remove the entire scene.#Incorrect. The scene can be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Add a random joke.#Incorrect. That may weaken suspense. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Add action beats as the character listens at the door and watches the light shift.#Correct. Action beats slow the moment and build suspense.
~D. Change every name.#Incorrect. Names do not control pacing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A story shifts accidentally from Mara's thoughts to Ellis's thoughts in the same paragraph. What is the likely problem?{
=A. Point of view control.#Correct. Accidental shifts in thoughts often create point-of-view confusion.
~B. Setting description.#Incorrect. The issue is not setting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Spelling only.#Incorrect. The issue is perspective control. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Title length.#Incorrect. The title is not the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which revision explanation is strongest?{
~A. I changed stuff and now it is better.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I added an action before the ending so readers understand why the character leaves.#Correct. This names the revision and reader effect.
~C. I made the story longer for no reason.#Incorrect. Length alone is not revision quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I changed the font.#Incorrect. Font does not explain narrative craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which story problem should be fixed before final convention polishing?{
~A. One missing comma in an otherwise clear final draft.#Incorrect. That is polishing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A correctly spelled character name.#Incorrect. That is not a problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A purposeful short sentence.#Incorrect. Purposeful style may not need correction. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The central conflict is unclear.#Correct. Major story meaning should be revised before final polish.
}
